---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DebtDescentAppCta from '../../components/DebtDescentAppCta.astro';
import DebtDescentTools from '../../components/DebtDescentTools.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../components/EmailCapture.vue';

// The coach-code request posts to the contact endpoint tagged "Coach code",
// the same stopgap as the calculator list signup. Sean sends each App Store
// offer code by hand; no code is ever printed on this page.
const codeEndpoint = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_CONTACT_ENDPOINT ?? '/contact.php';

const title = 'Debt Descent for coaches and counselors';
const description =
  'A debt payoff planner your clients can use without handing over a bank login. Free to use, one-time Pro, free Pro codes for counselors.';

// Rendered as the visible FAQ and serialized into FAQPage JSON-LD below.
// Facts checked against the app on 2026-09-20: payment groups, JSON export
// and the progress card are free; the what-if slider is Pro; no coach login.
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do my clients need an account?',
    a: 'No. There’s nothing to sign up for, and nothing is collected. If a client is signed in to iCloud, the app syncs between their own devices; if not, it works fully on the one device.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I see a client’s plan?',
    a: 'Not through the app. A client can export the whole plan as JSON, share the progress card, or send screenshots. There’s no coach dashboard and no shared workspace.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it recommend a debt management plan, or sell one?',
    a: 'No. It tracks whichever plan the client is on, a DMP included, and stays out of the choice. The DMP calculator on this site compares a quoted plan with paying on your own, fees counted.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work on Android or the web?',
    a: 'No. It’s iPhone, iPad, and Mac only. The free calculators on this site run in any browser, so a client on Android can still use those.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does it cost my client?',
    a: 'The app is free. Pro is $14.99, paid once, with no subscription, and if you work with clients you can ask me for a free code on this page.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are you connected to a counseling agency?',
    a: 'Only as a client. I’m on Consolidated Credit’s debt management plan myself, and I built Debt Descent to track it. No agency pays me, and none had a hand in the app.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map(({ q, a }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a },
  })),
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} appleAppId="6785269702">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm font-medium">
      <a href="/debt-descent/" class="no-underline text-accent-600 transition-colors hover:text-accent-700">Debt Descent</a>
      <span class="text-slate-400"> / For coaches and counselors</span>
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Debt Descent for coaches and counselors</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-xl font-medium text-slate-700">A payoff planner you can hand a client without asking for a bank login.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[65ch] text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
      If you’re an Accredited Financial Counselor, a counselor at an NFCC member agency, a Ramsey
      coach, or you coach people on money on your own, you’ve probably built the spreadsheet:
      balances, rates, minimums, a payoff order, a date. Debt Descent is that spreadsheet as a
      native app for iPhone, iPad, and Mac, kept up by the client between sessions. This page is
      what I’d want to know before recommending it to someone.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-14 max-w-[65ch]">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Why hand it to a client</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        The client enters each debt’s real balance, APR, and minimum, and the app shows Snowball
        and Avalanche side by side: the debt-free date on each, and the interest each one costs.
        The payoff order is on the screen, so the conversation about which method to pick happens
        with the client’s own numbers instead of an example.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        The Plan tab draws the descent to zero, and the extra payment is a field the client can
        change to watch the date move. Pro adds a what-if slider that redraws the plan as it’s
        dragged, which is the quickest way I know to answer “what if you found $50 more a month.”
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        There’s no account and no bank linking. The App Store privacy label is “Data Not
        Collected,” and I run no servers of my own, so recommending it never means asking a client
        to hand over credentials, and nothing a client enters reaches me.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        It’s one app for iPhone, iPad, and Mac, and a client’s data syncs between their devices
        through their own iCloud.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">It tracks a debt management plan exactly</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        Payment groups live on the Payments tab. The client enters the one monthly draft, the plan
        fee, and the debts it covers, and the app splits the payment across them to the cent,
        following the servicer’s own schedule when every member has a fixed payment. The Upcoming
        card captions what goes out and what reaches the creditors, as in “$837.00 leaves your
        bank ($32.00 plan fee),” so the app matches the bank statement while the fee stays out of
        the payoff math. Payment groups are free.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        The debts a plan leaves out keep their own place. A mortgage or a car payment goes in
        Income &amp; bills, so the Left over card shows what the month has after debts and bills. A
        student loan in deferment carries the switch “In deferment — nothing due yet” and a date,
        and it rejoins the plan on that date by itself. A card on a 0% promo takes the date the
        promo ends and the APR it reverts to, and the plan changes rates on schedule.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        If a client is weighing a plan, the free <a href="/debt-descent/dmp-calculator/" class="font-medium text-accent-600 transition-colors hover:text-accent-700">DMP calculator</a> runs the plan they’ve been quoted against paying on their own, and counts the fees.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What you can see</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        Whatever the client chooses to show you. The ••• menu has <strong>Export to JSON…</strong>,
        which writes the whole plan, every debt, payment, and setting, to a file the client can send
        you. The Progress tab has <strong>Share your progress</strong>, a card with the debt-free
        date, what’s left, and how far along they are, and screenshots work as they always have.
        There’s no coach login and no shared workspace. The app is built so that nobody but the
        client can see the data, and that includes me.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Free Pro for counselors</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        Debt Descent is free to use, and Pro is a one-time $14.99 purchase with no subscription,
        Family Sharing enabled. Pro adds five more payoff methods, one of them a custom order, the
        what-if slider and per-debt payoff tests, planned windfalls, refinance and consolidation
        modeling, amortization schedules, PDF and calendar export, and due-date reminders.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        If you work with clients, I’ll give you Pro free. Ask for a code:
      </p>
      <EmailCapture
        endpoint={codeEndpoint}
        source="/debt-descent/for-coaches/"
        event="debt-descent-coach-code"
        tag="Coach code"
        message="Please send me a Debt Descent Pro code for counselors. Asked on /debt-descent/for-coaches/."
        idPrefix="coach"
        buttonLabel="Ask for a code"
        sentLabel="Sent"
        successText="Thanks. I’ll send a code to that address."
        client:load
      />
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
        I send each one by hand as an App Store offer code, which redeems in the app’s Settings.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 max-w-[65ch]">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Questions coaches ask</h2>
      <dl class="mt-6 space-y-8">
        {faqs.map(({ q, a }) => (
          <div>
            <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-900">{q}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">{a}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
        Disclosure: I’m a current client of Consolidated Credit’s debt management plan, and I built
        Debt Descent to track it. Nobody pays me to recommend a DMP, and this page doesn’t sell one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <DebtDescentAppCta heading="Send a client to the App Store" event="debt-descent-coaches-app-store">
      Debt Descent is free on iPhone, iPad, and Mac. The <a href="/debt-descent/" class="font-medium text-accent-600 transition-colors hover:text-accent-700">app page</a> answers
      the questions people ask before they download, the <a href="/debt-descent/support/" class="font-medium text-accent-600 transition-colors hover:text-accent-700">support page</a> covers
      the ones that come up after, and the <a href="/debt-descent/dmp-calculator/" class="font-medium text-accent-600 transition-colors hover:text-accent-700">DMP calculator</a> is
      there for clients weighing a plan.
    </DebtDescentAppCta>

    <DebtDescentTools current="/debt-descent/for-coaches/" />

    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  </div>
</Layout>
